-- DICT-SEMANTICS §3 `inst` / §6 C1 / §6 "uniform resolution" / §10's last-but-one
-- bullet ("Suspect any resolution code path that ... KEYS INSTANCES BY
-- CLASS-PLUS-HEAD-CONSTRUCTOR ALONE, WHICH CANNOT SEPARATE `List Int` FROM
-- `List a`").
--
-- ✅ DRAINED -- **#1128** (S0: silent wrongness, `verified`), fixed by F-3b on
-- 2026-08-01. This file pinned the WRONG answer 99/99/99 on purpose from the day
-- the gate was built until then; it now pins the SPEC answer below, which was
-- hand-derived before the bug was even filed and is unchanged since.
-- History kept deliberately: the mechanism section is the useful part of this
-- comment and the trap it records nearly cost two more agents a session each.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived, before running anything; now also the OBSERVED one):
--   `match(IE, π)` = { I | ∃φ. φ(head(I)) = π } (§3). `head(impl Tag (Box Int))`
--   is `Box Int`; no substitution makes it `Box String` or `Box Bool`. So:
--     goal `Tag (Box Int)`    -> match {Tag a, Tag (Box Int)}, min⊑ = Box Int => 99
--     goal `Tag (Box String)` -> match {Tag a} ALONE                          => 10
--     goal `Tag (Box Bool)`   -> match {Tag a} ALONE                          => 10
--   Expected output: 99, 10, 10.
--
-- OBSERVED WHILE #1128 WAS OPEN: **99, 99, 99** -- on `check` (exit 0, no
-- diagnostic), on `run`, and on the shipped native binary alike. Two of the three
-- lines called an impl their goal does not match.
--
-- ⚠️ THE TRIGGER WAS THE GENERAL IMPL'S HEAD BEING A BARE TYPE VARIABLE. IT IS
-- INDEPENDENT OF THE TYPE CONSTRUCTOR -- `Box` here is incidental. Probed
-- directly, same interface, same two goals, ONE token different:
--     impl Tag a       + impl Tag (List Int)  ->  99 / 99   WRONG (pre-F-3b)
--     impl Tag (List a) + impl Tag (List Int) ->  99 / 10   CORRECT (always)
-- Both impls sit at the SAME tycon in the second, and it answered correctly even
-- while this file did not.
--
-- ⚠️ AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS COMMENT SAID THE MANGLED SYMBOL "DROPS THE TYPE
-- ARGUMENTS" AND THAT SELECTION IS "KEYED ON `Box`". THAT IS FALSIFIED BY THIS
-- CORPUS -- if arguments were dropped, the `List a` vs `List Int` row could not
-- answer 99/10. The symbols show what actually happens:
--     general head `a`      -> `@mdk_impl___none___tag` + `@mdk_impl_List_tag`
--     general head `List a` -> `@mdk_impl_Tag__List_a___tag`
--                            + `@mdk_impl_Tag__List_Int___tag`
-- When two impls share a head tycon the mangler disambiguates with the FULL head
-- and selection is correct. The short name in the buggy case is a SYMPTOM of the
-- head bucket having exactly one member, not the cause.
--
-- MECHANISM, in two tiers -- keep them apart, because collapsing them is the
-- mistake this comment already made once:
--
--   OBSERVED (pre-fix), off `medaka build --keep-ir`: all three call sites lowered
--   to one DIRECT call to the `Box Int` impl, and the general impl was emitted and
--   never called. Because the call is direct, the decision is taken during
--   ELABORATION, not at run time. Post-fix the same command shows the 3-site
--   partition the gate now pins by COUNT: two calls to `@mdk_impl___none___tag`,
--   one to `@mdk_impl_Box_tag`.
--
--   TRACED TO SOURCE by a later independent analysis (attributed, and both lines
--   re-read here before citing rather than taken on trust):
--   `headTyconTy` returns `None` for a `TyVar` -- its `TyCon`/`TyApp`/`TyTuple`
--   arms are the only ones that yield `Some`, and a bare type variable fell to
--   the catch-all. `keyEntryOf` then emitted a `KeyEntry` ONLY on `Some tag`, with
--   `None => []`. So an `impl C a` produced NO key entry at all: the candidate was
--   never collected into the registry the goal searches, rather than being
--   collected and then out-ranked. That is why the general impl was unreachable
--   for EVERY goal, not merely mis-ranked at one -- and it is a sharper statement
--   than the `univConcreteBucket`/`univHeadless` split this comment previously
--   offered as a lead, which is a DIFFERENT registry. Line numbers drift;
--   re-derive with `grep -n '^headTyconTy' compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`.
--
--   🚨 THE HALF THAT COST TWO AGENTS A SESSION EACH. Registering the entry and
--   unioning it into the lookups is NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT -- built on its
--   own it is INERT and this file stays at 99/99/99. A headless winner sits alone
--   in the `noneHeadTag` bucket, so `keyForSite`'s head-collision gate never fired,
--   it answered `None`, and `entailInst`'s `optionOr tag (…)` fell back to the
--   GOAL's head tycon -- discarding the correctly-selected candidate one line
--   after selecting it. F-3b's third part is that `else` arm. If you are probing
--   this area and your control will not move, that is where to look.
--
-- NOT #1072: that is runtime arm-matching, and it states "single-module
-- permutations are CLEAN", which this single-module program contradicts. NOT
-- #667 either: that was the FLAT shape (`impl Sz Int` + `impl Sz a`), was
-- interpreter-only, and its exact repro prints the intended 999/999 on both
-- engines here -- genuinely fixed.
--
-- 🔗 The target-architecture task most likely to SUBSUME this is **#1113**
-- (ARCH B-2, "identity-stamped evidence ... min-specificity only at `inst`,
-- emitter word-set retirement"). §11's arg-tag row names the same granularity
-- defect one layer down -- `runtimeTypeTag` discriminating at bare head-tycon
-- width, so it cannot separate `T Int` from `T Bool` either. That row is about
-- eval's `filterByTag` RUNTIME fallback; this fixture's call is a DIRECT call
-- decided at elaboration, so they are different sites of one keying assumption.
-- Recorded as a pointer, not a dedup claim: #1128 is the tracked entry.
--
-- 🔬 THE CONTROL. `s3-min-fully-general-control.mdk` is THE ONE-TOKEN control:
-- byte-identical to this file except the general impl's head reads `(Box a)`
-- instead of `a`. It answers 99 / 10 / 10 -- correctly.
-- ⚠️ `s5-argtag-unsound-under-overlap.mdk` is NOT the one-token control, though an
-- earlier revision of this comment claimed it was. It differs in the type
-- constructor (`List` vs `Box`), the general head, AND the number of call sites,
-- so it cannot isolate a single variable. It remains a useful SECOND data point
-- (the bug reproduced at `List` too), just not a control.
-- `s3-nested-obligation-most-specific.mdk` is a third data point at parametric
-- width. So the trigger was a bare-type-variable general head beside a
-- tycon-headed impl -- not overlap as such, and not declaration order.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ROW NOW PINS THE SPEC ANSWER, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS PINNING WHAT THE
-- ENGINE DOES. `99\n10\n10` was written into this comment before the fix existed
-- and was not recaptured from the fixed binary; the fix was graded against it, not
-- the other way round. If this row ever goes red again, re-derive from §3 first --
-- do not bless.
data Box a = Box a

interface Tag a where
  tag : a -> Int

impl Tag a where
  tag _ = 10

impl Tag (Box Int) where
  tag _ = 99

main =
  println (tag (Box (1 : Int)))
  println (tag (Box "s"))
  println (tag (Box True))
